    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters July 5th with a clear focus on aggressive base-running and high-floor match-ups. The top play of the day is undoubtedly **James Wood** (76.27), who benefits from an elite 0.420 xwOBA and a favorable matchup against Chandler. Despite a 1.86s pop-time catcher threat, his ability to capitalize on the pitcher's slow delivery makes him a primary target for stolen base production. Closely following is **Mickey Moniak** (75.97) and **Brice Turang** (75.05). **Mickey Moniak** benefits from a massive 14% park boost and strong platoon leverage against Mahle, while **Brice Turang** utilizes an elite 19% SP skill advantage and his leadoff spot to maximize scoring potential in a category where we remain hyper-focused on speed.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved **Cole Young**, whose 47.90 score nearly forced a rotation change. Ultimately, the upside of **Zach McKinstry** in the outfield\u2014despite his own efficiency concerns\u2014held firm due to the premium placed on speed-adjacent roles, leaving **Cole Young** on the bench alongside **Austin Riley**, who saw his score suppressed to 46.90 by a difficult matchup against McLean. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** (72.02) remains a locked-in start, even with a minor 4% penalty for facing a pitcher capable of holding runners. \n\nWe are monitoring the status of **Gabriel Moreno** (59.22), whose projection is stabilized by a clean lineup spot, and **Travis Bazzana** (50.71), who faces the toughest uphill battle of the day due to a -9% penalty from a L/L platoon disadvantage. While the bottom of our lineup, including **Max Muncy** (55.43) and **Kyle Karros** (56.81), sits in the marginal tier, we are prioritizing the cumulative efficiency of our speed-heavy core over pure power. We continue to hold high-upside bench assets like **Jose Caballero**, **Edwin Arroyo**, and **Trevor Story** in reserve, prepared to pivot if late-breaking weather or lineup shifts create unexpected volatility for our primary starters.",
